in A does exactly that: it iterates c from 0 to 255 and updates best only when counts[c] > best_count, so ties keep earlier (smaller) c.\n\nThus we can simply:\n- Count frequencies of real heap labels.\n- Add (k - heap_size) to count[255] if char is unsigned? But what is the actual value of -1 as a char? In C, assigning -1 to char yields implementation-defined; on ARM with default signedness? Let's check: On AArch64, GCC defaults `char` to unsigned unless `-fsigned-char`. The compiler flags likely don't specify. So it's probably unsigned, so -1 becomes 255. But we should not assume; we can compute the sentinel label as (char)-1 and use its unsigned value: `(unsigned char)(char)-1`. That will be either 255 or 127? Actually if char is signed, -1 remains -1 which as unsigned char is 255 too because two's complement. So it's always 255 when cast to unsigned char. Good.\n\nSo we can add missing count to freq[255].\n\nNow for NEON: We need to include <arm_neon.h>. The target architecture is ARM64, so NEON available. We'll use vld1q_f32 on xs and ys arrays (since SoA). For each group, process j in steps of 4:\n```\nfloat32x4_t qx = vdupq_n_f32(to_evaluate.x);\nfloat32x4_t qy = vdupq_n_f32(to_evaluate.y);\nfor (j=0; j+3<g.length; j+=4) {\n    float32x4_t xs_v = vld1q_f32(&g.xs[j]);\n    float32x4_t ys_v = vld1q_f32(&g.ys[j]);\n    float32x4_t dx = xs_v - qx;\n    float32x4_t dy = ys_v - qy;\n    float32x4_t d2 = dx*dx + dy*dy;\n    float d[4];\n    vst1q_f32(d, d2);\n    for (t=0; t<4; t++) {\n        // heap insert with idx\n    }\n}\n```\nThen scalar remainder.\n\nWe need to ensure alignment? vld1q doesn't require 16-byte alignment but may be faster if aligned. We can allocate xs/ys with malloc, which returns at least 8-byte aligned typically; for NEON we might want 16-byte alignment. Could use posix_memalign or just rely on compiler to handle unaligned loads (vld1q supports unaligned). It's fine.\n\nNow OpenMP: We could parallelize over groups, but each group has its own